        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser introduced a genuinely new route to semiconductor laser emission and opened important infrared and terahertz capabilities.",
        "attribution": "Faist and Capasso are central to the original quantum-cascade concept and realization; Cho's contribution should be assessed against the enabling epitaxial-device history.",
        "maturity": "The devices are experimentally secure, technologically mature, and widely deployed.",
        "uncertainties": ["Credit for the original invention versus enabling semiconductor heterostructure technology needs exact archival review."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Jagadeesh S. Moodera", "Stuart S. P. Parkin", "Terunobu Miyazaki"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance transformed spintronics and enabled the magnetic-memory technologies that followed.",
        "attribution": "Moodera and Miyazaki made crucial early room-temperature demonstrations, while Parkin's work was central to the practical and scientific development.",
        "maturity": "The phenomenon is robustly replicated and its technological consequences are unambiguous.",
        "uncertainties": ["Independent near-contemporaneous demonstrations make priority and the appropriate three-person division especially important."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hideo Hosono", "Xianhui Chen", "Zhongxian Zhao"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Iron-based high-temperature superconductivity was a major unexpected materials discovery that created a durable research field.",
        "attribution": "Hosono's original discovery is particularly clear; Chen and Zhao are associated with prompt confirmation and extension, but their precise priority must be distinguished.",
        "maturity": "The superconducting phase family, its broad phenomenology, and its importance are firmly established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The proper line between the initial discovery and rapid subsequent discoveries by several groups needs careful attribution."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Christian J. Bordé", "David E. Pritchard", "Mark A. Kasevich"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry supplied a powerful new instrument for measuring gravity, rotation, and fundamental quantities with exceptional sensitivity.",
        "attribution": "Bordé developed key conceptual foundations and Pritchard and Kasevich made seminal experimental contributions, though credit spans a broad community.",
        "maturity": "The methods are extensively validated and have developed into reliable precision-measurement platforms.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field has multiple early experimental and theoretical lines, so an exclusive three-person attribution may be difficult."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
